### How does range allocation remove the central bottleneck?

Each application server fetches a block of counter values from a central allocator, then mints codes locally from that block without any network call. When the block is exhausted, it fetches the next one.

```java
package com.bank.shortener.service;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;

public class RangeAllocator {

    private final AtomicLong globalCounter = new AtomicLong(1_000_000);
    private static final long RANGE_SIZE = 10_000;

    public long[] allocateRange() {
        long start = globalCounter.getAndAdd(RANGE_SIZE);
        return new long[] { start, start + RANGE_SIZE - 1 };
    }
}

// Each server instance:
// long[] range = allocator.allocateRange(); // [1000000, 1009999]
// Mint codes from range[0] to range[1] locally.
// When exhausted, fetch the next range.
```

### How does hashing compare to counter-based generation?

| Aspect | Counter + Base62 | Hash + Truncate |
|---|---|---|
| Uniqueness | Guaranteed, zero collisions | Collisions possible, needs detection |
| Same URL = same code | No (unless you check) | Yes (by construction) |
| Central dependency | Range allocator (infrequent) | None |
| Complexity | Simple | Collision retry logic needed |

```java
package com.bank.shortener.service;

import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public class HashBasedGenerator {

    public static String generateCode(String longUrl) throws Exception {
        MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
        byte[] hash = md.digest(longUrl.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        // Take first 7 chars of base62-encoded hash
        long value = Math.abs(java.nio.ByteBuffer.wrap(hash).getLong());
        String code = Base62Encoder.encode(value);
        return code.substring(0, Math.min(7, code.length()));
    }

    // PROBLEM: two different URLs can produce the same 7-char code.
    // Must check DB and retry with a salt on collision.
}
```

### How does the cache-first redirect path work?

The same popular short codes are requested constantly. A Redis cache absorbs the majority of reads.

```java
package com.bank.shortener.service;

public class RedirectService {

    private final RedisCache cache;
    private final UrlMappingRepository repository;

    public RedirectService(RedisCache cache, UrlMappingRepository repository) {
        this.cache = cache;
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public String resolve(String shortCode) {
        // 1. Check cache first (sub-millisecond)
        String longUrl = cache.get(shortCode);
        if (longUrl != null) {
            return longUrl;
        }
        // 2. Cache miss: read from DB, populate cache
        longUrl = repository.findByCode(shortCode);
        if (longUrl != null) {
            cache.set(shortCode, longUrl, 3600); // TTL 1 hour
        }
        return longUrl;
    }
}
```

### What is the difference between 301 and 302 redirects?

HTTP 301 (permanent) tells browsers to cache the mapping. Future clicks skip your server, which is fastest but loses per-click analytics. HTTP 302 (temporary) brings every click back, enabling counting and re-pointing.

```java
package com.bank.shortener.controller;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import java.net.URI;

public class RedirectController {

    private final RedirectService redirectService;
    private final AnalyticsPublisher analyticsPublisher;

    // Use 302 for campaign links (need analytics)
    public ResponseEntity<Void> redirect(String shortCode) {
        String longUrl = redirectService.resolve(shortCode);
        if (longUrl == null) {
            return ResponseEntity.notFound().build();
        }
        // Record click asynchronously (off the hot path)
        analyticsPublisher.recordClick(shortCode);
        // 302 = every click comes back to us for counting
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FOUND)
            .location(URI.create(longUrl))
            .build();
    }
}
```

### How do you handle custom aliases and analytics?

Custom aliases go through the same uniqueness constraint. Analytics are recorded off the hot path by emitting a click event to Kafka.

```java
package com.bank.shortener.service;

public class CustomAliasService {

    private final UrlMappingRepository repository;

    public String createCustomAlias(String alias, String longUrl) {
        if (repository.existsByCode(alias)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Alias already taken: " + alias);
        }
        // Validate against reserved words: "api", "admin", "health"
        if (isReserved(alias)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Reserved word: " + alias);
        }
        repository.save(alias, longUrl);
        return alias;
    }

    private boolean isReserved(String alias) {
        return java.util.Set.of("api", "admin", "health").contains(alias);
    }
}
```

### How do you scale the key space?

Base62 with 7 characters gives 62 to the power of 7, which is about 3.5 trillion codes. Size the length to exceed projected volume with headroom. Scale throughput by keeping services stateless, sharding the mapping store by code, and relying heavily on the cache.

```text
# Key space calculation:
# 62^5  =        916,132,832   (~1 billion)
# 62^6  =     56,800,235,584   (~57 billion)
# 62^7  =  3,521,614,606,208   (~3.5 trillion)
# For 1 billion URLs, 7 characters is more than enough.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How would you handle URL expiry and reclaim codes? How do you prevent abuse (someone creating millions of short URLs)? How would you implement A/B testing with different redirect destinations? What happens when the cache goes down? How would you migrate from 6-character codes to 7-character codes without breaking existing links?
